    faqTitle: 'Frequently asked <span class="accent">questions</span>',
    faqLead: "A quick overview of how cooperation works. If you don't see your question, drop us a line — we reply within a working day.",
    faq: [
      { group: "Basic concepts", q: "What is occupational health and safety in simple terms?", a: "Occupational health and safety is a system for protecting the life and health of employees in the course of their work. It is not just paperwork, but a set of measures: from proper organization of workplaces and provision of workwear to training and medical examinations. In Kyrgyzstan it is regulated by the Labour Code and the dedicated Law on Labour Protection. The employer's task is to create conditions that minimise the risk of injuries and occupational diseases at work." },
      { group: "Basic concepts", q: "What is industrial safety and how does it differ from occupational health and safety?", a: "Industrial safety is the protection of vital personal and public interests from accidents at hazardous production facilities (for example at mines, plants, and energy facilities) and their consequences. While occupational health and safety focuses on each employee and their actions during the working day, industrial safety focuses on the technical condition of equipment, buildings, and production processes as a whole to prevent major accidents." },
      { group: "Basic concepts", q: "What is fire safety?", a: "Under the Kyrgyz Republic Law on Fire Safety, it is the state of protection of individuals, property, society, and the state from fires. It includes legal, organisational, and technical measures: from having fire extinguishers and evacuation plans to complying with electrical equipment operating rules and conducting fire safety briefings." },
      { group: "Basic concepts", q: "What is environmental protection?", a: "It is a system of state and public measures aimed at harmonious interaction between society and nature, and at the conservation and reproduction of natural resources. For businesses in Kyrgyzstan this means compliance with environmental standards: from obtaining permits for emissions and waste disposal to conducting mandatory environmental impact assessments for projects." },
      { group: "Legislation and responsibilities", q: "Is an employer required to assess occupational risks?", a: "Yes. Both the Labour Code and the Law on Labour Protection require employers to ensure the assessment of occupational risks. However, Kyrgyz law does not set a strict frequency (such as once every 5 years), so we recommend assessing risks regularly, especially when technological processes change or new equipment is introduced. NORVEX helps conduct risk assessments and document the results." },
      { group: "Legislation and responsibilities", q: "What are an employer's main obligations to employees and the state?", a: "<p>The employer is obliged to:</p><ul><li>Ensure safe working conditions at every workplace.</li><li>Provide quality PPE (workwear, footwear) free of charge.</li><li>Conduct safety briefings and knowledge checks on occupational health and safety.</li><li>Organise mandatory medical examinations.</li><li>Conduct assessments of occupational risks and certification of workplaces.</li><li>Insure employees against workplace accidents.</li></ul><p>To the state, the obligation is to comply with all standards, submit reports to supervisory authorities on time, and eliminate identified violations.</p>" },
      { group: "Legislation and responsibilities", q: "Is it true that an OHS specialist is only needed if there are more than 50 employees?", a: "Yes, that is the key threshold. In organisations with more than 50 employees engaged in production activities, establishing an OHS service or introducing a specialist position is mandatory. If there are 50 or fewer employees, the employer decides. However, if you choose not to create a position, you are still required to ensure safety — in that case the law allows you to enter into an outsourcing contract with a specialised organisation (such as NORVEX)." },
      { group: "Legislation and responsibilities", q: "Who must pay for workwear and personal protective equipment (PPE)?", a: "All costs are borne by the employer. You may not transfer the cost of PPE to employees or deduct it from wages. The employer covers not only purchase but also storage, laundering, repair, and decontamination of PPE. We can help develop PPE issuance standards and accounting procedures." },
      { group: "Legislation and responsibilities", q: "Can a mandatory medical examination be replaced by an oral interview?", a: "No, that is a direct violation. Preliminary (on hiring) and periodic medical examinations must be carried out in state healthcare institutions. An oral interview has no legal validity, and work without a medical examination is prohibited. NORVEX helps organise the medical examination process and maintain the relevant documentation." },
      { group: "Legislation and responsibilities", q: "Is an evacuation plan required if the premises are small and the fire risk is low?", a: "Yes, it is required. The law obliges all protected facilities (regardless of ownership form and size) that are subject to fire safety requirements to display floor evacuation plans. An employer's view that the risk is low does not override this requirement. We develop evacuation plans and fire safety documentation." },
      { group: "Consequences and risks", q: "What happens if requirements for occupational health and safety, fire and environmental safety are not met?", a: "<p>Consequences fall into three groups:</p><ul><li><strong>Financial:</strong> heavy fines for the company and officials. For example, fines for environmental violations in the Kyrgyz Republic are being tightened, and supervisory authorities actively conduct inspections.</li><li><strong>Administrative:</strong> suspension of operations (for example, stopping work at a dangerous section or on equipment) until violations are eliminated.</li><li><strong>Criminal and personal:</strong> in case of an accident with a serious outcome or major environmental damage, the manager may face criminal liability as well as full material liability for harm to an employee's health.</li></ul>" },
      { group: "Consequences and risks", q: "What does a company face for a workplace accident?", a: "In addition to fines, the manager may bear full financial liability for harm to the employee's health. In the Kyrgyz Republic employer liability is being tightened, including mandatory certification of workplaces and strict oversight of compliance. NORVEX helps prepare the company for inspections and minimise risks." },
      { group: "Benefits and international standards", q: "What does a company gain from complying with safety requirements besides avoiding fines?", a: "<p>Beyond protection from fines, you gain:</p><ul><li><strong>Reputation:</strong> a company where safety is at a high level is more attractive to investors and partners.</li><li><strong>Reduced costs:</strong> fewer sick days, fewer insurance cases, lower staff turnover.</li><li><strong>Competitiveness:</strong> compliance with international standards (for example, ISO 45001) opens doors to cooperation with major international corporations and participation in tenders.</li></ul>" },
      { group: "Benefits and international standards", q: "What is ISO certification and what types are there?", a: "<p>ISO is the International Organization for Standardization. ISO certification confirms that a company's management system meets world requirements. For your sector the most relevant are:</p><ul><li>ISO 9001 — quality management.</li><li>ISO 14001 — environmental management.</li><li>ISO 45001 — occupational health and safety management (replaced the former OHSAS 18001).</li></ul><p>The difference: it is not just a certificate, but confirmation that your processes (from PPE procurement to accident response) are built systematically and aimed at continuous improvement.</p>" },
      { group: "Benefits and international standards", q: "What is the difference between ISO 14001 and ISO 45001?", a: "ISO 14001 focuses on how your company affects the external environment (emissions, waste, resource consumption). ISO 45001 focuses on the internal environment — on how you protect employees from injuries and occupational diseases at work. Implementing both systems (an integrated management system) gives the maximum effect for reaching the international level." },
      { group: "Outsourcing and outstaffing", q: "How does outsourcing differ from outstaffing of OHS?", a: "With outsourcing we take over the functions of your OHS service: conducting audits, developing documentation, organising processes, and representing your interests before government bodies. With outstaffing we provide you with a qualified specialist who actually works on your site and reports to your management, but is employed by NORVEX. This allows you to quickly meet the need for an OHS engineer without the costs of creating a workplace and paying taxes." },
      { group: "Outsourcing and outstaffing", q: "If we outsource occupational health and safety, who is liable if an accident occurs?", a: "Liability to the state and to the injured employee always rests with the employer (your company). However, if NORVEX provided services of poor quality (for example, failed to identify a hazardous factor that was within our contractual duties), we bear liability under the contract and compensate the damage. Our task is to ensure that labour inspectors have no claims against you." },
      { group: "Outsourcing and outstaffing", q: "Will your specialist come to our site or work remotely?", a: "The format depends on the contract. With outsourcing, a NORVEX specialist regularly visits the site to conduct audits, check documentation, and assess working conditions. With outstaffing, the specialist is on your site in line with your work schedule. We do not work blindly — for a quality result, access to real working conditions is needed." },
      { group: "Outsourcing and outstaffing", q: "We already have an OHS engineer, but they cannot cope with the workload (or are going on leave). Can you help?", a: "Yes. We offer temporary outstaffing (for example for the period of parental leave or illness of a full-time specialist) or can take over individual functions (for example, only the audit, only documentation, or only inspection support). This is often cheaper and faster than hiring a temporary employee." },
      { group: "Outsourcing and outstaffing", q: "We already work with another OHS contractor. Why switch to you?", a: "We offer a risk-based approach and independent expertise. NORVEX does not just close paperwork — we identify real risks and help build a system so that labour inspectors have no claims. If you are unsure, we can conduct a one-off audit of the current situation and show where the weak points are." },
      { group: "Documentation, audit and inspections", q: "How can you help if we have no documentation on occupational health and safety at all?", a: "We will conduct an audit, develop a full package of required documents (instructions, logs, orders, regulations), set up an accounting system, and train your employees to keep documentation correctly. All in line with the requirements of Kyrgyz legislation." },
      { group: "Documentation, audit and inspections", q: "Do we need to update OHS instructions if the equipment has changed?", a: "Yes, necessarily. OHS instructions must be reviewed at least once every 5 years, and earlier if the technological process changes, equipment is replaced, or supervisory bodies require it. NORVEX can update your instructions and bring them into line with new Kyrgyz standards." },
      { group: "Documentation, audit and inspections", q: "What is included in an occupational health and safety audit and how long does it take?", a: "The audit includes checking documentation, assessing working conditions at workplaces, identifying violations and risks, and recommendations for eliminating them. Time depends on the size of the site and the number of workplaces — usually from a few days to two weeks. As a result you receive a report with an action plan." },
      { group: "Documentation, audit and inspections", q: "Do you help pass a State Labour Inspectorate inspection?", a: "Yes. We can conduct a pre-inspection audit, prepare the necessary documents, and support you during the inspection — representing the company's interests and helping to promptly address remarks." },
      { group: "Documentation, audit and inspections", q: "Do you develop documentation on fire safety and environmental protection?", a: "Yes. NORVEX develops fire safety documentation (evacuation plans, instructions, orders) and also helps with documentation on environmental protection and industrial safety in line with Kyrgyz requirements." },
      { group: "Documentation, audit and inspections", q: "Do you work across Kyrgyzstan?", a: "Yes. Our specialists travel to sites across the republic. We support companies in Bishkek, Osh, Jalal-Abad, and in remote regions, including industrial and mining sites." },
      { group: "Specialist requirements (education and qualifications)", q: "What education must an OHS specialist have?", a: "An OHS specialist must have higher professional education in Occupational Health and Safety, Technosphere Safety, or a related engineering specialty, or higher education plus professional retraining in OHS. Without relevant education or retraining, a person cannot hold the position of OHS specialist. In addition, periodic knowledge checks in OHS are required." },
      { group: "Specialist requirements (education and qualifications)", q: "What education is needed for an industrial safety specialist?", a: "Work in industrial safety requires higher technical education matching the profile of the hazardous production facility (mining, energy, metallurgy, chemicals, etc.), as well as training and certification in industrial safety. The specialist must know the requirements for hazardous production facilities and be able to organise production control." },
      { group: "Specialist requirements (education and qualifications)", q: "What education is required for an environmental specialist (ecologist)?", a: "As a rule, higher education in Ecology, Environmental Protection, Nature Management, or a related specialty (chemistry, biology, geography — depending on the industry) is required. Knowledge of Kyrgyz environmental legislation and of procedures for obtaining permits and submitting reports is also important." },
      { group: "Specialist requirements (education and qualifications)", q: "What education is needed for a fire safety specialist?", a: "Higher or secondary professional technical education is required, preferably in Fire Safety, or special training (retraining) in fire safety. The specialist must know fire safety requirements, be able to develop documentation, and organise fire safety measures." },
      { group: "Specialist requirements (education and qualifications)", q: "Can one person be responsible for OHS, industrial safety, environmental protection, and fire safety at the same time?", a: "Formally, combining roles is possible if the employee meets the qualification requirements for each area (education plus training/certification). In practice this is rarely correct: the areas require different knowledge and training. It is better to have separate specialists, or outsourcing/outstaffing from us, where we cover all areas with profile experts." },
      { group: "Specialist requirements (education and qualifications)", q: "Is advanced training sufficient if the specialist has non-profile education?", a: "No, training alone is not enough. If the education is not profile-specific, professional retraining (usually from 250 hours) in the relevant field is required, granting the right to work in that field. Advanced training courses supplement existing profile education rather than replace it." },
      { group: "Specialist requirements (education and qualifications)", q: "How does NORVEX verify the qualifications of its specialists?", a: "We engage specialists with profile education and confirmed training in the areas (OHS, industrial safety, environmental protection, and fire safety). Before admission to a project we check documents on education, retraining, and certification, as well as relevant experience in the client's industry. For complex sites (mining, hazardous production facilities) we select experts with the corresponding technical profile." },
    ],
